description.narrative:
The project addresses urgent and growing risks faced by children and youth in Thailand due to increased internet use. While 85% of young people use social media daily and 81% own a smartphone or tablet, this digital access exposes them to significant harm. Alarming statistics reveal that:

- Up to 69% have experienced cyberbullying;  
- 11% have faced online sexual harassment;  
- 36% have been targeted by online grooming, with even higher rates among girls (41%) and LGBTIQ youth (50%);  
- 9% of internet-using children aged 12–17 were victims of severe online sexual exploitation in the past year.

Children also report negative impacts on family life and school performance due to excessive screen time (35+ hours/week). Exposure to harmful content is widespread—60% of pornography seen by children is child sexual abuse material. Thailand ranks 6th globally in hosting such content.

Cultural norms and victim-blaming further discourage reporting: up to 31% of victims do not disclose abuse, often due to lack of awareness or fear of stigma. Girls and young women are disproportionately affected, and boys also face harmful gender expectations. These trends highlight the urgent need for targeted, gender-sensitive interventions to protect children online and offline.
